course: dictionary
lesson: 6588
title: Nitrogen, the gas in the air
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Nitrogen is a gas. You cannot see it, smell it or taste it, but it is all around you. Nearly four fifths of the air is nitrogen, so you breathe it in and out every time you breathe, without using it up.
explain: The sentence «Air is about one fifth oxygen and nearly four fifths nitrogen» tells you how much there is. Nitrogen is the biggest part of the air, and oxygen is the second biggest. Scientists write the symbol for nitrogen as N.
explain: Context helps you understand why nitrogen is used in food packets. «Nitrogen hardly reacts, so crisp packets are filled with it to keep the crisps fresh.» Hardly reacts means it does not easily mix with other things or change them, so the crisps stay crisp.
fact: Nitrogen is a gas with no colour, no smell and no taste.
fact: About 78 parts in every 100 parts of air are nitrogen.
fact: The symbol for nitrogen is N.
fact: «Nitrogen hardly reacts, so crisp packets are filled with it.»
fact: Liquid nitrogen is extremely cold, about −196 °C, so only scientists handle it.
exercise: What is the symbol for nitrogen? | Ni | Na | *N | O
exercise: «Air is about 78 parts nitrogen, 21 parts oxygen and 1 part other gases in 100.» What is the biggest part of the air? | Carbon dioxide | Argon | *Nitrogen | Oxygen
exercise: Why are crisp packets filled with nitrogen? | To make the crisps heavy | *Because nitrogen hardly reacts, so it keeps the crisps fresh | To make the crisps glow | To make the crisps cold
test: What colour, smell and taste does nitrogen have? | Sour, like lemons | Blue, like the sky | *None: it has no colour, no smell and no taste | Sweet, like sugar
test: Which description of liquid nitrogen is correct? | *Extremely cold, about −196 °C | Warm, like a cup of tea | Room temperature | Hot enough to melt metal
exam: Plants need nitrogen to grow, but they do not take it straight from the air. Where do plants get the nitrogen they need? | Straight from the air through their leaves | *From the soil, as nitrates | From rainwater only | From sunlight
